        <h3 id="character">What living here is like</h3>
        <p>Walton combines three things that explain much of its modern appeal: <strong>London access, a functioning town centre and the River Thames</strong>.</p>
        <p>It is not one uniform suburban area. The historic streets around St Mary's and the river feel very different from the modern centre around The Heart. Ashley Park has a different housing character again, while the station and Halfway area are more convenient for rail commuters. Cottimore and Sidney Road preserve the layout of older estates, and north Walton has its own mixture of housing, sport and open space.</p>
        <p>That variation matters. A general description of Walton can only take a prospective resident so far, which is why the next section goes area by area.</p>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/history/st-marys-church-walton-on-thames-new-zealand-flag.webp"
            alt="The tower of St Mary's Church, Walton-on-Thames, framed by spring blossom, with the New Zealand flag flying from the flagpole"
            loading="lazy"
            width="1200"
            height="1200"
            sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px"
          />
          <figcaption>St Mary's Church stands at the heart of the Church Street conservation area. The New Zealand flag flies from its tower each April, a legacy of the town's First World War hospital at Mount Felix. Photograph by Darren Bayley.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" data-areas="walton-on-thames" data-primary="walton-on-thames" data-relevance="primary">
        <h2 id="areas">Explore the areas</h2>
        <p>Walton-on-Thames has no official neighbourhood boundaries. The six below are practical local descriptions, covered in full on our areas page. Hersham and Whiteley Village have their own sections further down, because neither is a Walton neighbourhood.</p>

        <h3 id="walton">Walton-on-Thames</h3>
        <p>There is no single official set of neighbourhood boundaries for the town. These six are <strong>practical local descriptions, not formal administrative divisions</strong>. Each is covered in full, with its streets drawn on a map, in our <a href="/living/areas/">guide to the areas of Walton-on-Thames</a>.</p>

        <div class="areas-summary">
          <ol class="area-list" role="list">
            {areas.map((area) => (
              <li class="area-item">
                <span class="area-number" style={`--area: ${area.colour}`} aria-hidden="true">{area.number}</span>
                <div>
                  <h3><a href={`/living/areas/#${area.id}`}>{area.name}</a></h3>
                  <p class="area-where">{area.where}</p>
                  <p class="area-text">{area.text} ({area.cite}).</p>
                </div>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>

          <figure class="areas-map-thumb">
            <a href="/living/areas/#map">
              <img
                src="/images/maps/walton-areas-map.svg"
                alt="Map of Walton-on-Thames with the six practical local areas numbered 1 to 6 along the streets each one names"
                loading="lazy"
                width={mapData.width}
                height={mapData.height}
              />
            </a>
            <figcaption><a href="/living/areas/#map">Open the areas map</a>, with conservation areas and the flood-warning area as optional layers.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <p>Hersham is a neighbouring village rather than a seventh area of Walton: see <a href="/hersham/living/">living in Hersham</a>.</p>

        <h3 id="geography">What counts as Walton-on-Thames?</h3>
        <p>This is more complicated than the postal address suggests.</p>
        <p>The Office for National Statistics defines a <strong>Walton-on-Thames built-up area</strong> as a statistical geography. Census 2021 data can therefore be reported for that geography, but it is not the same thing as the KT12 postcode district, current council wards or the historic parish (Office for National Statistics, 2023).</p>
        <p>The <strong>KT12 postcode district is broader than Walton-on-Thames</strong>. It includes Hersham and Whiteley Village addresses: Whiteley Homes Trust gives its own address as Octagon Road, Walton on Thames, KT12 4EH, and sold-price records for streets in Hersham such as Southdown Road and Queens Road carry KT12 postcodes with Walton-on-Thames as the postal town (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.a; Rightmove, 2026). Hersham is a distinct village with its own centre and identity, while Whiteley Village is a distinct residential community south of Hersham. Both are covered by this site because of their close practical links with the town, but neither should simply be counted as a Walton neighbourhood.</p>
        <p>Walton-on-Thames railway station adds another complication. It serves Walton and north Hersham, and sits much closer to some residential streets than it does to Walton town centre (Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026a).</p>
        <p>For anyone comparing properties, the most useful unit is therefore the <strong>actual street and immediate neighbourhood</strong>, not the postal town or the postcode.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" data-areas="hersham" data-primary="hersham" data-relevance="primary">
        <h2 id="hersham-guide">Hersham</h2>
        <p>Hersham is a village in its own right, not a district of Walton. It has its own green and centre, its own shops and surgery, its own primary schools and its own station, and it keeps a separate identity that long predates the shared KT12 postal address (Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026b).</p>

        <h3 id="hersham-centre">The village centre</h3>
        <p>Everyday shopping is centred on the parade around Hersham Green and the Hersham Centre, with a supermarket, cafes and takeaways alongside the pharmacy and Post Office. It is a village centre rather than a town centre: for a full range of shops, most people go into Walton, Kingston or Weybridge.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/hersham/">The Hersham guide</a> · <a href="/hersham/food-and-drink/">food and drink in Hersham</a></p>

        <h3 id="hersham-station">Hersham station</h3>
        <p>Hersham has its own smaller station, on the same South West Main Line as Walton-on-Thames but with a different stopping pattern and a less frequent service. Two trains an hour run each way, all of them stopping services, and every London train takes about 33 minutes to Waterloo because the fast lines through the middle of the station have no platforms. Times are approximate and change with the timetable, stopping patterns and engineering work (Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026d; National Rail, 2026b).</p>
        <p>The station has no lift and no step-free route to the platforms, which matters for anyone with mobility needs, a pushchair or heavy luggage (National Rail, 2026b). Walton-on-Thames station also serves the west of the village and has the faster and more frequent service.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/hersham-railway-station/">Hersham station guide</a> · <a href="/walton-on-thames-railway-station/">Walton-on-Thames station guide</a></p>

        <h3 id="hersham-schools">Schools and surgery</h3>
        <p>Three primary schools sit within the village, Bell Farm, Burhill and Cardinal Newman, with Three Rivers Academy on Bell Farm Way as the secondary serving it. Hersham Surgery is on Pleasant Place, off the green, with two pharmacies in the village. Our Hersham living guide covers all of them with addresses, age ranges and links to each school's Ofsted record, and it is the page to use rather than this summary.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/hersham/living/">Living in Hersham: schools, doctors, getting around and council services</a></p>

        <h3 id="hersham-getting-around">Getting around</h3>
        <p>Buses link the village to Walton, Esher, Kingston, Shepperton, Staines and Whiteley Village, including routes 458 and 459, with 564 and 663 serving local and school journeys. Surrey County Council publishes the current routes and timetables, which change often enough that reproducing them here would date quickly (Surrey County Council, 2026a).</p>
        <p>By road the A244 runs through the village between Esher and Walton, and the A317 links west towards Weybridge, with M25 Junction 10 a few miles east.</p>

        <h3 id="hersham-housing">Housing and families</h3>
        <p>Hersham has no house-price index of its own. The official figures on this page are for the whole borough of Elmbridge, and commercial portals fold Hersham addresses into their Walton-on-Thames results, because Hersham uses Walton-on-Thames as its postal town: sold entries for Southdown Road, Vaux Crescent and Queens Road all appear that way (Rightmove, 2026). For a purchase here, street-level completed sales are the only reliable comparison.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/living/community/?area=hersham">Clubs and groups in Hersham</a> · <a href="/hersham/development-and-planning/">development and planning in Hersham</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" data-areas="whiteley-village" data-primary="whiteley-village" data-relevance="primary">
        <h2 id="whiteley-guide">Whiteley Village</h2>
        <p><strong>Whiteley Village is a charitable retirement community, not open-market housing.</strong> It is owned and run by the Whiteley Homes Trust, it cannot be bought or rented in the ordinary way, and people move there by applying to the Trust and meeting its eligibility rules. Nothing on this page sets those rules: they are the Trust's, and the Trust decides who qualifies.</p>
        <p>The village sits south of Hersham in its own grounds, laid out around a series of concentric octagons, and has been a designated conservation area since 1979. It was built with the fortune left by the Bayswater department-store owner William Whiteley for older people of limited means, and that is still who it houses.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/hersham/whiteley-village/">The history and architecture of Whiteley Village</a> · <a href="https://www.thewhiteleyhomestrust.org.uk/living-at-whiteley" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Living at Whiteley, Whiteley Homes Trust ↗</a></p>

        <h3 id="whiteley-apply">Who can apply</h3>
        <p>The Trust publishes its criteria, and they are stricter than most people expect. At the time of checking, applicants must be 65 or over if male and 60 or over if female, with a net annual income of no more than £25,000 for a single person or £40,000 for a couple, and capital of no more than £30,000 per person or £50,000 per couple. Applicants must have lived in the UK for five or more consecutive years, and must show a housing need, such as being at risk of involuntary homelessness, a medical or welfare need made worse by their present home, or insecurity in a private shorthold tenancy. The Trust also states that it does not currently accept applicants with a dementia diagnosis (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.c).</p>
        <p><strong>These are the Trust's rules and they change.</strong> Check the current criteria on the Trust's own page before making any decision, and treat this summary as a pointer rather than an authority.</p>

        <h3 id="whiteley-how-to-apply">How to apply</h3>
        <p>Applications go directly to the Whiteley Homes Trust on its own forms, not through an estate agent, a letting agent or the council. The Trust asks for an application form, an equality monitoring form and a references consent form, completed in full with supporting documentation, and publishes a list of current vacancies (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.c).</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="https://www.thewhiteleyhomestrust.org.uk/living-at-whiteley/apply-to-live-at-whiteley" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apply to live at Whiteley, Whiteley Homes Trust ↗</a></p>

        <h3 id="whiteley-accommodation">Accommodation and what it is called</h3>
        <p>The village provides more than 250 one and two-bedroom almshouse cottages, together with 51 self-contained extra-care flats at Huntley House (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.b; n.d.d). The Trust's pages currently give slightly different counts for the cottages, so this guide uses the cautious figure until the Trust confirms one.</p>
        <p>The word to understand here is <strong>almshouse</strong>. These are not tenancies in the ordinary sense and they are not properties for sale, so the usual language of buying, renting, deposits and landlords does not apply. Cottages are offered unfurnished, residents arrange and pay for their own utilities and service contracts, and the Trust provides a maintenance and repairs service (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.d).</p>

        <h3 id="whiteley-life">Life and facilities in the village</h3>
        <p>The village has its own shop and Post Office, open Monday to Friday mornings and early afternoons and on Saturday mornings, selling groceries, fresh produce and bakery deliveries alongside Post Office services (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.e). The Whiteley Community Café in the Clubhouse on Octagon Road is open every day and is open to everyone, not only residents (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.f). There is also a swimming pool, and the Trust runs a volunteering programme (Whiteley Homes Trust, n.d.g).</p>
        <p>Those are the facilities the Trust publishes, and this guide names only those. For anything else, including clubs, activities and worship, ask the Trust.</p>
        <p>The grounds are private and residential. The café is the part most visitors use.</p>

        <h3 id="whiteley-health">Healthcare and pharmacies</h3>
        <p><strong>Healthcare is accessed outside the village.</strong> No GP surgery with a Whiteley Village address appears in our GP directory, and the Trust's residents' handbook directs residents towards Hersham Surgery, with Walton Health Centre and nearby pharmacies also serving the village (Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026e).</p>
        <p>For emergencies, St Peter's Hospital in Chertsey has the 24-hour Accident and Emergency department (Ashford and St Peter's Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust, 2026a). Walton Community Hospital provides local health services, including Fort House Surgery, but it is not an A&E (NHS, n.d.e; NHS, n.d.f).</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/living/gp-surgeries/">GP surgeries</a> · <a href="/living/pharmacies/">pharmacies</a> · <a href="/living/dentists/">dentists</a></p>

        <h3 id="whiteley-transport">Getting to and from the village</h3>
        <p>Bus route <strong>459</strong> is the village's public transport link, running between Staines, Laleham, Shepperton, Walton, Hersham and Whiteley Village, and stopping at "Stores, Whiteley Village" (Surrey County Council, 2026a). Timetables change, so check the current one before relying on a particular journey.</p>
        <p>Otherwise a car, taxi or bicycle from Walton or Hersham is the practical way in. The nearest stations are Walton-on-Thames and Hersham, both of which need a bus, car or taxi from the village.</p>

        <h3 id="whiteley-costs">Costs and what to check</h3>
        <p>The borough house-price and rent figures elsewhere on this page <strong>do not describe Whiteley Village</strong>. Almshouse accommodation is not sold or let on the open market, so those numbers are irrelevant to a move here.</p>
        <p><strong>What living at Whiteley Village costs is for the Trust to set out, not this guide.</strong> Contact the Whiteley Homes Trust directly for the current charges and what they cover. It is also the place to ask about:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>which utilities and services residents arrange and pay for themselves;</li>
          <li>Council Tax, and which band the accommodation falls in;</li>
          <li>the occupancy terms, including what an almshouse appointment means in practice and the circumstances in which it can end;</li>
          <li>what happens if care needs increase, and how Huntley House differs from a cottage; and</li>
          <li>current vacancies and how long the waiting list is.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="https://www.thewhiteleyhomestrust.org.uk/living-at-whiteley" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Living at Whiteley, Whiteley Homes Trust ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.thewhiteleyhomestrust.org.uk/contact-us" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Contact the Trust ↗</a></p>
        <p>The general moving checklist further down this page is written for people buying or renting on the open market. Much of it, including surveys, leases and landlord checks, does not apply to an almshouse appointment.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" data-areas="walton-on-thames hersham" data-primary="walton-on-thames hersham" data-relevance="shared">
        <h2 id="housing">Housing and the real cost of living</h2>
        <p>Walton sits within an expensive borough housing market, but no single headline figure accurately represents every part of the town.</p>

        <h3>Official Elmbridge market context</h3>
        <p>The Office for National Statistics, using UK House Price Index data from HM Land Registry, reported an <strong>average Elmbridge house price of £748,000 in July 2026</strong>, up 1.9% from July 2025. ONS marks these local figures as provisional, and they are revised as more sales complete (Office for National Statistics, 2026).</p>
        <p>In the same release, the average price paid by first-time buyers in Elmbridge was <strong>£501,000</strong>, while home movers paid an average of <strong>£956,000</strong> (Office for National Statistics, 2026).</p>
        <p>Those figures are useful context, but they cover the whole borough. Elmbridge includes Walton, Weybridge, Esher, Cobham, the Moleseys, the Dittons and other settlements with different property markets.</p>

        <h3>Why this guide does not quote a "Walton average" from a property portal</h3>
        <p>Commercial portals publish useful sold-price data, but their geography does not always match the town itself.</p>
        <p>Current Rightmove results labelled <strong>Walton-on-Thames</strong> include properties in Hersham, because Hersham commonly uses Walton-on-Thames as its postal town. Sold entries for Southdown Road, Vaux Crescent and Queens Road, all in Hersham, appear under the Walton-on-Thames heading. A single portal average can therefore mix more than one local market (Rightmove, 2026).</p>
        <p>For that reason, this guide does not present a portal average as an official Walton-only figure. For a purchase, the most useful evidence is <strong>recent completed sales of genuinely comparable homes on the same street or in the same immediate area</strong>.</p>
        <p>A future property page on this site should calculate a reproducible Walton-only measure directly from HM Land Registry Price Paid Data, using a clearly documented geographic definition.</p>

        <h3>Renting</h3>
        <p>Official rental statistics are also published at Elmbridge level rather than for Walton. The average private rent across Elmbridge was <strong>£1,891 a month in August 2026</strong>, up 2.6% from £1,843 a year earlier (Office for National Statistics, 2026).</p>
        <p>Average monthly rents by bedroom number were (Office for National Statistics, 2026):</p>
        <ul>
          <li>one bedroom: <strong>£1,264</strong></li>
          <li>two bedrooms: <strong>£1,589</strong></li>
          <li>three bedrooms: <strong>£1,937</strong></li>
          <li>four or more bedrooms: <strong>£2,884</strong></li>
        </ul>
        <p>These are borough-wide averages, not expected rents for an individual Walton property.</p>

        <h3 id="costs">Council Tax</h3>
        <p>For the 2026/27 financial year, total Council Tax for an <strong>Elmbridge Band D property outside Claygate Parish is £2,556.87</strong> (Elmbridge Borough Council, 2026a).</p>
        <p>That is the total charge published by Elmbridge Borough Council for the borough outside Claygate Parish. The amount due depends on the individual property's valuation band, and properties within Claygate Parish are charged separately (Elmbridge Borough Council, 2026a).</p>
        <p>For commuters, rail fares and parking can also be significant household costs. Anyone moving specifically for the London connection should price the journey they will actually make, including office frequency, ticket type, station access and onward travel in London.</p>

        <h3 id="monthly-costs">What a monthly budget has to cover</h3>
        <p>Beyond the mortgage or rent, a realistic monthly figure for this area needs to allow for Council Tax at the property's own band, utilities and broadband, buildings and contents insurance, the full commute including any station parking and onward travel in London, childcare if relevant, and car ownership where a household keeps one. A flat or a house on an estate may also carry a service charge or other lease obligations, which are property-specific and need checking before an offer.</p>
        <p>This guide does not publish an example household budget, because the numbers that would make it useful, the exact band, tariff, ticket, nursery and lease, are all property-specific. The checks in <a href="#property-checks">before choosing a property</a> are the ones that turn those into real figures.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" data-areas="walton-on-thames" data-primary="walton-on-thames" data-relevance="primary">
        <h2 id="transport">Getting around and commuting</h2>
        <p><strong>Direct trains run from Walton-on-Thames to London Waterloo. The fastest scheduled services are usually around 22 to 25 minutes, while many stopping services take around 30 to 35 minutes.</strong> The pattern varies by time of day, at weekends and during engineering work (South Western Railway, 2026; Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026a).</p>
        <p>The headline rail time is only part of the commute. The station is roughly a <strong>20 to 25-minute walk from Walton town centre</strong>, and total journey time varies substantially depending on the street you live on and where in London you need to reach (Walton-on-Thames.org, 2026a).</p>
        <p>The station also has direct services towards Weybridge and Woking, with onward connections across Surrey and the wider south-west rail network (National Rail, 2026a).</p>

