        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/walton-on-thames-railway-station/">Complete station guide: platforms, tickets, parking and accessibility</a> · <a href="/getting-here/">Getting here by road, rail and bus</a></p>

        <h3 id="getting-around">Buses, roads and getting around</h3>
        <p>Surrey County Council's current Walton-area timetable list includes (Surrey County Council, 2026a):</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>400/813</strong>, linking Walton with Shepperton, Charlton Village, Ashford and Staines;</li>
          <li><strong>458</strong>, linking Walton with Hersham, Esher, Thames Ditton and Kingston in one direction, and Shepperton, Laleham and Staines in the other;</li>
          <li><strong>459</strong>, linking Walton with Hersham and Whiteley Village, and with Shepperton, Laleham and Staines; and</li>
          <li><strong>461</strong>, linking Walton with Weybridge, Addlestone, St Peter's Hospital and Chertsey in one direction, and West Molesey, Hampton Court and Kingston in the other.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Routes and timetables change, so this page deliberately does not reproduce departure times.</p>
        <p>By road, Walton connects towards Weybridge, Esher, Molesey, Chertsey, the A3 and the M25. Peak journey times vary substantially, so a fixed claim such as "ten minutes to the motorway" is less useful than checking the journey at the time you would normally travel.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" data-areas="walton-on-thames hersham" data-primary="walton-on-thames hersham" data-relevance="shared">
        <h2 id="schools">Schools, nurseries and childcare</h2>
        <p>Families should treat school choice as an <strong>address-specific admissions question</strong>, not a town-wide promise. Admission arrangements are set by each school and published in Surrey County Council's admission booklets, and Surrey's school map shows how far a school is from your home and whether it has a catchment area, so an individual address can materially affect priority (Surrey County Council, 2026b).</p>
        <p>Walton and the immediately surrounding area have a mixture of state and independent primary schools, with secondary options in Walton's neighbouring communities as well as the wider Elmbridge area.</p>
        <p>Use our directory as a starting point, then check admission criteria, school places and inspection outcomes against Surrey County Council, the school itself and Ofsted before making a decision.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="/living/schools/">Schools serving Walton-on-Thames</a> · <a href="/hersham/living/">Schools in Hersham</a></p>

        <h3 id="early-years">Nurseries, childminders and funded childcare</h3>
        <p>Early years provision is separate from school admissions and is organised differently. Surrey County Council runs the Family Information Service, whose childcare directory lists Ofsted-registered nurseries, preschools and childminders by postcode, and explains the types of childcare and how funded places work (Surrey County Council, 2026d).</p>
        <p>Funded hours are a national scheme rather than a local one. The government's Best Start in Life service describes 15 and 30 hours support for children aged between 9 months and 4 years, alongside Tax-Free Childcare and Universal Credit childcare support, and provides a checker that estimates what a family qualifies for and finds providers nearby. Eligibility rules and start dates change, so the checker is the source to use rather than a figure repeated here (Best Start in Life, 2026a).</p>
        <p>Two practical points are worth knowing before a move. A nursery near a chosen house is not a place: popular settings in this area operate waiting lists, and families often register well before they need the place. And funded hours do not always cover a full working day, so the gap between funded hours and actual working patterns is usually filled by paid extra hours, which vary by provider.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="https://www.surreycc.gov.uk/children/support-and-advice/families/childcare" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Childcare in Surrey, Surrey County Council ↗</a> · <a href="https://childcare.beststartinlife.gov.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Childcare checker, Best Start in Life ↗</a></p>

        <h3 id="wraparound">Breakfast clubs, after-school and holiday care</h3>
        <p>For school-age children, the working day rarely matches the school day. The government's Best Start in Life service covers free breakfast clubs and wraparound childcare for primary-school-aged children, which is the national framework schools work within (Best Start in Life, 2026b).</p>
        <p>What each school actually offers, and at what cost, differs school by school, so the school's own website and office are the reliable source. Holiday provision is separate again and is usually run by private providers or clubs rather than by the school itself.</p>
        <p class="deeper-link"><a href="https://beststartinlife.gov.uk/childcare-early-years-education/breakfast-clubs-and-wraparound-childcare/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Breakfast clubs and wraparound childcare, Best Start in Life ↗</a></p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" data-areas="walton-on-thames hersham" data-primary="walton-on-thames hersham" data-relevance="shared">
        <h2 id="everyday">Everyday life</h2>
        <h3 id="healthcare">GPs, dentists, pharmacies and healthcare</h3>
        <p>Walton has several NHS GP practices. The Red, White and Yellow Practices operate from Walton Health Centre on Rodney Road, Fort House Surgery is at Walton Community Hospital on the same road, and Ashley Medical Practice is on Crutchfield Lane (NHS, n.d.a; NHS, n.d.b; NHS, n.d.c; NHS, n.d.d; NHS, n.d.f). Hersham has its own surgery. Practices open, merge and close their lists, so our <a href="/living/gp-surgeries/">GP surgeries directory</a> is the maintained list rather than this paragraph.</p>
        <p>Registration status changes, so our GP directory links through to the NHS service used to check whether a practice is currently accepting patients, rather than freezing that into this page.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/living/gp-surgeries/">GP surgeries in Walton-on-Thames and Hersham</a></li>
          <li><a href="/living/pharmacies/">Pharmacies</a></li>
          <li><a href="/living/dentists/">Dentists</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p>For hospital care, <strong>St Peter's Hospital in Chertsey</strong> provides a 24-hour Accident and Emergency service, and the trust lists Walton-on-Thames among the main centres of population the hospital serves (Ashford and St Peter's Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust, 2026a; 2026b). Walton Community Hospital provides local health services but does not list an A&E department (NHS, n.d.e).</p>

        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" data-areas="walton-on-thames hersham whiteley-village" data-primary="walton-on-thames" data-relevance="primary">
        <h2 id="shopping">Shopping, food and everyday life</h2>
        <p>Walton has a proper town centre rather than a commuter station surrounded by housing.</p>
        <p>The Heart, High Street, Bridge Street, Church Street and New Zealand Avenue provide a mixture of shops, supermarkets, cafes, restaurants, pubs and services (The Heart Shopping Centre, 2026). The exact mix changes, which is why this site maintains live directories rather than freezing a business list into a guide.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/shopping/">Shopping in Walton-on-Thames</a></li>
          <li><a href="/food-and-drink/">Food and drink</a></li>
          <li><a href="/directory/">Business directory</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Kingston upon Thames, about seven miles away, has a far larger range of shops, and is the retail centre most people here name for a bigger shopping trip. Weybridge and Hersham provide additional nearby options.</p>

        </section>

        <section class="guide-section" data-areas="walton-on-thames hersham whiteley-village" data-primary="walton-on-thames" data-relevance="primary">
        <h2 id="outdoors">The Thames, parks and outdoor life</h2>
        <p>The Thames is one of Walton's defining features and one of its main public spaces.</p>
        <p>The <strong>Thames Path</strong> passes through Walton. Surrey County Council describes the walk upstream from Walton Bridge towards the Shepperton Ferry as a level surfaced path (Surrey County Council, 2026c). Cowey Sale provides riverside open space near <a href="/history/walton-bridge/">Walton Bridge</a>, and walking routes continue along the river in both directions.</p>
        <p>The river supports rowing, boating and the Walton and Weybridge Regatta, whose organisers describe the event as formerly Walton Amateur Regatta and as held annually since 1862 (Walton and Weybridge Regatta, n.d.). Away from the Thames, Ashley Park and local recreation grounds provide further green space, while sports facilities are concentrated around Waterside Drive and elsewhere across the town (Elmbridge Borough Council, n.d.b).</p>

